The LINUX64 machine layer: XNU hosted inside a Linux kernel by xnu.ko.

The CPU, MMU and exceptions are Linux's: XNU runs at EL1 on Linux's page tables (its kernel L1 entries installed by the
host), with hardware interrupts masked and a virtual interrupt flag; exceptions are taken in Linux's vectors and handed
back (kernel faults to linux64_kernel_fault, user space through linux64_el0_run/linux64_el0_exception). Idle, timers,
the console, printf/kprintf, panics and failed asserts go to the host; entropy comes from the host until corecrypto
registers. Every hosted path is behind `if (linux64_hook)`: the same kernel still boots on hardware.

The interface to the host is linux64/linux64_abi.h (shared with xnu.ko) and linux64/linux64.h, copied into
EXTERNAL_HEADERS/linux64 from xnu-patches/include by scripts/vm-xnu-rebuild.sh.

